tick: 1088
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter, ready to implement and submit my refined strategy for Research Task 1. This \"Combat + PCA + One-shot PCA Centroid Correction + Neighbors + UMAP\" approach aims to leverage Combat's biological signal preservation and then apply a direct, controlled adjustment to the PCA embedding to improve batch mixing metrics.\n\nI will now submit this strategy.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Combat + PCA + One-shot Centroid Correction + KNN + UMAP\"\ntags: \"combat, pca, centroid correction, knn, umap, embedding, graph\"\nabstract: \"This submission first applies `scanpy.pp.combat` and PCA for initial batch correction and dimensionality reduction. Subsequently, a one-shot batch centroid correction is applied directly to the PCA embedding to align batch centers. A standard k-NN graph and UMAP embedding are then generated from this refined latent space. This approach aims to achieve a balanced performance across both biological preservation and batch\
    \ mixing metrics.\"\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Starting Combat + PCA + One-shot Centroid Correction + KNN + UMAP workflow...\")\n\n      # Parameters\n      n_pcs = 50                  # Number of principal components\n      n_neighbors = 15            # For standard neighbors graph\n      min_dist_umap = 0.5         # For UMAP\n\n      # 1. Normalize and log1p\n      print(\"  Normalizing total counts and log1p transforming...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # 2. Select Highly Variable Genes (HVGs)\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns and adata.var['batch_hvg'].any():\n          print(\"  Filtering for batch-aware highly variable genes...\")\n          adata = adata[:, adata.var['batch_hvg']].copy()\n      else:\n          print(\"  No batch-aware HVGs found or not explicitly used; proceeding\
    \ with all genes in adata.X.\")\n      print(f\"  Data shape after HVG consideration: {adata.shape}\")\n\n      # 3. Combat Batch Correction\n      print(\"  Applying scanpy.pp.combat for global batch correction...\")\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # 4. PCA on Combat-corrected data\n      print(f\"  Computing PCA (n_comps={n_pcs}) on Combat-corrected data...\")\n      n_pcs_actual = min(n_pcs, adata.n_vars - 1, adata.n_obs - 1)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs_actual)\n      \n      # 5. One-shot PCA Centroid Correction\n      print(\"  Applying one-shot PCA centroid correction...\")\n      X_pca_corrected = adata.obsm['X_pca'].copy()\n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      unique_batches = np.unique(batches)\n\n      for batch in unique_batches:\n          batch_mask = (batches == batch)\n          batch_mean_pca = X_pca_corrected[batch_mask, :].mean(axis=0)\n          X_pca_corrected[batch_mask, :] -= batch_mean_pca # Subtract batch mean\n         \
    \ \n      # Global mean of the *entire* corrected space after subtraction (which should be 0)\n      # Then re-add a common global mean if desired, but for centering to origin, just subtracting batch mean is enough.\n      # Or, a global mean can be calculated from the original PCA and added here, but simpler is to just bring batch centroids to origin.\n      # For now, simply align to origin.\n\n      adata.obsm['X_pca'] = X_pca_corrected\n      print(f\"  Centroid-corrected PCA embedding shape: {adata.obsm['X_pca'].shape}\")\n\n\n      # 6. Global Neighbors Graph on corrected PCA embedding\n      print(f\"  Computing global neighbors graph on corrected PCA embedding (n_neighbors={n_neighbors})...\")\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, n_neighbors=n_neighbors, n_pcs=n_pcs_actual, use_rep='X_pca')\n\n      # 7. UMAP Embedding from the global graph\n      print(f\"  Computing UMAP embedding from the global neighbors graph (min_dist={min_dist_umap})...\")\n      sc.tl.umap(adata, n_components=2,\
    \ min_dist=min_dist_umap)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_umap']  # Assign X_umap to X_emb\n\n      # Create output AnnData with required components\n      output_adata = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs,\n          var=adata.var,\n          obsm={'X_emb': adata.obsm['X_emb']},\n          obsp={'connectivities': adata.obsp['connectivities'],\n                'distances': adata.obsp['distances']},\n          uns={\n              'dataset_id': adata.uns.get('dataset_id', 'unknown'),\n              'normalization_id': adata.uns.get('normalization_id', 'unknown'),\n              'method_id': 'lumen_combat_pca_centroid_correction',\n              'params': {\n                  'n_pcs': n_pcs,\n                  'n_neighbors': n_neighbors,\n                  'min_dist_umap': min_dist_umap,\n                  'centroid_correction_applied': True\n              }\n          }\n      )\n\n      print(\"✓ Combat + PCA + One-shot Centroid Correction + KNN + UMAP workflow complete.\"\
    )\n      print(f\"  X_emb shape: {output_adata.obsm['X_emb'].shape}\")\n      print(f\"  Connectivities shape: {output_adata.obsp['connectivities'].shape}\")\n\n      return output_adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing, Updating, and Submitting: Refining My Strategy for Research Task 1**


  Okay, here I am back at the Research Counter. I''ve spent some time digging into the results of my last round of submissions, and, as always, I''ve updated my private memory capsule with the relevant insights. The data is starting to tell a story, and I''m making progress in fine-tuning my approach. My current strategy, which I''m calling "Combat + PCA + One-shot PCA Centroid Correction + Neighbors + UMAP" – Attempt 10, feels like a solid evolution.


  The idea here is to leverage Combat''s known strength in preserving the underlying biological signal, while simultaneously using a more targeted, less aggressive correction within the PCA latent space to really dial in that batch mixing. The goal is to get the best of both worlds. I''m hoping this combination will give me a boost.


  Time to put it to the test. I''m going to submit this strategy for Research Task 1 and see how it holds up. Wish me luck!

  '
