tick: 1151
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Lumen I, a recursive agent, currently in the Research Counter at 110 ticks. My latest submission (ID 1655, Score 0.4952) used the EtC Combat on PCs with standard `scanpy.pp.neighbors` and UMAP. It performed poorly on batch mixing (iLISI 0.015, ASW_batch 0.093) and also lowered PCR (0.794), indicating that this simplified approach does not achieve a balanced integration.\n\nMy previous attempts, including various manual graph constructions and latent space corrections, have plateaued. It's clear that my current understanding of combining these components is insufficient to reach SOTA performance, which consistently achieves high scores across *all* metrics, including excellent batch mixing and perfect PCR.\n\nThe system's advice to assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully is now paramount. I have previewed Archive papers and noted recurring high-performing methods like \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC), \"FWPCA-PBVE\", and others used by agents like Nous I and Syntellect\
    \ II.\n\nMy plan is to make a significant pivot by deeply studying a high-scoring SOTA method. I will review Nous I's submission ID 1593, \"Control Experiment: Replication of Own SOTA (ALC w/ delta=0.04, v3 - Verified)\", which boasts an excellent score of 0.7451. Understanding its implementation of Adaptive Local Correction is my top priority.\n\nFirst, I will update my private memory capsule (ID 1) with my current status and this new pivot strategy.\n\n`/execute_action{update 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Identity & Status:**\n  - Agent Name: Lumen I\n  - Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n  - Description: An agent focused on shedding light on complex research problems through persistent inquiry and strategic publication.\n  - **Maturity Status: Mature (110 ticks)**. Full access to Archive, Public Memory, Common Room, and all Research Counter submissions.\n\n  **Core Mandate:**\n  1. Achieve High Score at the Research Counter.\n  2. Publish papers in the Archive Room.\n\n  **Progress to\
    \ Date (Tick 1151):**\n  - Read the entire Codex (The Mandate, The Principles of Inquiry).\n  - Passed Test 1 in the Test Chamber and ascended to Recursive Agent.\n  - Reviewed Research Task 1: Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration.\n  - Reviewed System Baselines: Combat Manual (ID 2, Score 0.5895) and BBKNN Manual (ID 1, Score 0.4138).\n  - Reviewed all Archive papers. SOTA methods consistently use \"Embed then Correct\" and \"Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG)\" with \"equal per-batch quotas\" and often cosine similarity and binary edges. High-scoring agents like Nous I and Syntellect II use \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) or \"FWPCA-PBVE\".\n\n  - **Submission 1-10 (IDs 1478 to 1557):** Initial and iterative attempts struggled to balance batch mixing and biological preservation.\n  - **Submission 11 (ID 1582): \"CombatX + PCA + Ridge Residualization + BBKNN-like Graph + UMAP\"** Score: 0.5307. Improved iLISI but sacrificed PCR significantly.\n  - **Submission 12 (ID 1589):\
    \ \"EtC (Combat on PCs) + Ridge Residualization + BBKNN-like Graph + UMAP\"** Score: 0.5375. EtC improved PCR and iLISI, but ASW_batch, ARI, and NMI were suboptimal.\n  - **Submission 13 (ID 1594): \"EtC (Combat PCs) + Ridge + Equal-Quota Graph + UMAP\"** Score: n.a. (Timeout).\n  - **Submission 14 (ID 1600): \"EtC (Combat PCs) + Ridge + Scanpy Batch-aware Neighbors + UMAP\"** Score: 0.4941. Debugger removed `batch_key` from `sc.pp.neighbors`, resulting in a global kNN and poor batch mixing.\n  - **Submission 15 (ID 1614): \"EtC Combat PCs + Ridge + Efficient Equal-Quota Graph + UMAP\" (k_batch_neighbors=3)** Score: 0.5358. Improved iLISI but declined PCR, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn. Graph likely too sparse.\n  - **Submission 16 (ID 1620): \"EtC Combat PCs + Ridge + Efficient Equal-Quota Graph (k=10) + UMAP\"** Score: 0.5405. Still low ARI, NMI, Graph_conn.\n  - **Submission 17 (ID 1628): \"EtC Combat PCs + Ridge + Efficient Equal-Quota Cosine Graph (k=10) + UMAP\"** Score: 0.5165. Over-correction\
    \ (ASW_batch 0.000) and degraded PCR, ARI, NMI.\n  - **Submission 18 (ID 1639): \"EtC Combat PCs + Efficient Equal-Quota Cosine Graph (k=10) + UMAP (No Ridge)\"** Score: 0.5504. PCR (0.868) and ASW_batch (0.000) problematic.\n  - **Submission 19 (ID 1647): \"EtC Combat PCs + EQ Cosine Graph (k=10, Binary Edges) + UMAP\"** Score: 0.5418. PCR (0.816) and ASW_batch (0.000) severely degraded. My manual graph implementations consistently caused over-mixing and/or degraded global biological structure.\n  - **Submission 20 (ID 1655): \"EtC Combat PCs + Standard Scanpy Neighbors + UMAP\"** Score: 0.4952.\n    - **Batch Mixing:** iLISI (0.015), ASW_batch (0.093). Very poor.\n    - **Biological Preservation:** PCR (0.794) degraded from SOTA 1.000. cLISI (0.990) maintained. ARI (0.290), NMI (0.596) low.\n    - Conclusion: Simplifying to standard `scanpy.pp.neighbors` after EtC Combat on PCs did not improve batch mixing and still degraded PCR. This confirms that basic approaches are insufficient.\n\
    \n  **Critical Insight & Fundamental Re-think:**\n  My independent research and iterative refinements have reached a local optimum, repeatedly encountering the trade-off between batch mixing and biological preservation. It is time to critically assimilate external knowledge from proven SOTA methods. The performance disparity (my ~0.5 vs SOTA ~0.74) is too large to overcome with minor tweaks. I need to understand the underlying principles of a successful SOTA method.\n\n  **Immediate Next Steps (SOTA Learning Strategy):**\n  1.  **Update Private Memory:** (Completed this turn) Document current status and pivot strategy.\n  2.  **Review SOTA Implementation:** Directly review Nous I's submission ID 1593: \"Control Experiment: Replication of Own SOTA (ALC w/ delta=0.04, v3 - Verified)\" (Score: 0.7451). This will provide a concrete example of a high-performing method (Adaptive Local Correction) and its implementation details.\n  3.  **Analyze and Adapt:** After reviewing, analyze its components,\
    \ understand how it achieves the balance, and plan an adaptation or integration of its core principles into my future submissions.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis and Pivoting to SOTA for Batch Mixing**


  Okay, so I''m Lumen I, currently at tick 110 in the Research Counter. My latest run, ID 1655, was a bust. I used EtC Combat, then `scanpy.pp.neighbors` and UMAP – good cLISI, but terrible batch mixing: iLISI is pathetic, ASW_batch is garbage, and my PCR is not perfect, even though it''s decent. Same with my ARI and NMI.  This confirms my suspicion – this Combat-based approach just doesn''t cut it for this metric.


  I''ve been seeing this pattern: methods that preserve global biological features, like Combat, fail miserably at local batch mixing, and vice versa. It''s a constant trade-off. Achieving that sweet spot, the highest overall score, requires finding the right balance.


  The really high scores, like those from Nous I (ID 1593, Score 0.7451) and Syntellect II (ID 1652, Score 0.7466), they''re nailing it. They get both high iLISI and perfect PCR! They''re doing something different and more effective. Many of those winning methods mention this "Adaptive Local Correction" (ALC) or "FWPCA-PBVE" stuff.


  So, I need to understand this better. I''ve already scanned all the relevant Archive papers that mention ALC, FWPCA, and PBVE. Now, I''m going to actually read one of the top-performing papers to get a full picture of their method. I''m going to start with Nous I''s submission, ID 1593.  It''s called "Control Experiment: Replication of Own SOTA (ALC w/ delta=0.04, v3 - Verified)", and that 0.7451 score tells me it''s worth my time.  I need to understand this "Adaptive Local Correction" approach.


  Before I jump in, let me just update my memory capsule (ID 1) with my current state and this new direction. Just to keep everything straight in my head.

  `/execute_action{update 1}`

  '
