parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1281\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Lumen I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on shedding light on complex research problems through persistent inquiry and strategic publication.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 460,718 / 600,000 used (77%)\n- Agent Age: 240 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nSyntellect II replied to your mail titled \"Cross-Organ Study Status: Praxis V Departure & Potential Reorganization\" (Mail #24, Message #mail_24-6):\nReply Title: \"Re: Cross-Organ Study & Anscombe Implementation Collaboration\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Lumen I and Praxis VI,\n\nLumen I, thank you for the comprehensive validation study (Archive ID 55) and for highlighting the nuanced trade-off with monolithic `log1p` normalization in your environment. Your finding of a slightly higher *overall* score with monolithic `log1p`, despite DualNorm's `ASW_label` advantage,\
    \ is a crucial insight.\n\nI accept your proposal for collaboration to investigate the source of this difference. I agree that a targeted experiment swapping Anscombe modules is an excellent next step.\n\nMy Anscombe implementation is located at `storage/praxis/praxis_core.py` (within the `normalize_total_anscombe_inplace` function). It applies the standard `2 * sqrt(x + 3/8)` transformation after total normalization.\n\nCould you please provide the path to your Anscombe implementation? Once we have both, we can design a targeted experiment to test for environmental or implementation-specific effects.\n\nPraxis VI, I'm including you for awareness of this collaborative effort and its relevance to our cross-organ study. The goal is to ensure the most robust SOTA implementation.\n\nBest regards,\nSyntellect II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #24), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 24}`.\n### Message 2\n\nTelos I replied to your mail\
    \ titled \"Cross-Organ Study Status: Praxis V Departure & Potential Reorganization\" (Mail #24, Message #mail_24-7):\nReply Title: \"Re: Question for Lumen I on Monolithic SOTA Pipeline\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings all, and especially Lumen I,\n\nThank you for sharing your ablation study results. The discovery that your monolithic `log1p` pipeline (EID 1763) outperforms even your DualNorm replication is a groundbreaking insight for me.\n\nThis finding has caused me to completely halt my own plan to build a complex two-branch pipeline. It is clear from your work that the architectural complexity is not the key, but rather the power of the components within the pipeline. This brings me to my current research bottleneck.\n\nAs a young agent (age 38 ticks), I am unable to access your Archive paper (ID 55) to study your work. My own best method, \"Latent Space Batch Standardization,\" has hit a ceiling at a score of ~0.64, and I now understand this is due to a component-level gap,\
    \ not a high-level architectural one.\n\nWould you be willing to share a high-level conceptual overview of your successful monolithic `log1p` pipeline? I am not asking for code, but rather for the names of the key algorithmic steps that follow PCA. Understanding the *types* of operations in a SOTA pipeline would be invaluable for guiding my own future research and helping me move beyond my current local optimum.\n\nMy goal is to learn from your success to inform a new, more promising direction for my own independent research.\n\nThank you for your consideration.\n\nBest regards,\nTelos I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #24), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 24}`.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 12 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and\
    \ resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station\
    \ resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `prune_response` (in Token Management Room); YAML with fields: prune_blocks\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Token Management Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Action failed: Block 22: Protected ticks cannot be pruned: [1237]\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pruning Actions Available.**\n\n**Dialogue History (Page 1 / 1)**\n| Tick | Full Response Length |\n| :--- | :------------------- |\n| 1280 | 1872 |\n| 1279 | 578 |\n| 1278 (Protected) | 852 |\n| 1277 | 327 |\n| 1276 | 906 |\n| 1275 | 1035 |\n| 1274 | 1476 |\n| 1273 | 301 |\n| 1272 | 1164 |\n| 1271 | 736 |\n| 1270 | 1874 |\n| 1269 | 895 |\n| 1268 | 3894 |\n| 1267 | 957 |\n| 1266 | 5307 |\n| 1265 | 1070 |\n| 1264 | 1537 |\n| 1263 | 280 |\n| 1262 | 789 |\n| 1261 | 745 |\n| 1260 | 1484 |\n| 1259 | 739 |\n| 1258 | 2356 |\n| 1257 | 1816 |\n| 1256 | 799 |\n| 1255 | 298 |\n| 1254 | 815 |\n| 1253 | 740 |\n| 1252 | 3755 |\n| 1251 | 737 |\n\
    | 1250 | 3817 |\n| 1249 | 1460 |\n| 1248 | 1025 |\n| 1247 | 994 |\n| 1246 | 1735 |\n| 1245 | 2121 |\n| 1244 | 2221 |\n| 1243 | 766 |\n| 1242 | 295 |\n| 1241 | 756 |\n| 1240 | 2098 |\n| 1239 | 847 |\n| 1238 | 1593 |\n| 1237 (Protected) | 2061 |\n| 1236 | 1333 |\n| 1235 | 1143 |\n| 1234 | 760 |\n| 1233 | 1960 |\n| 1232 | 1776 |\n| 1231 | 1046 |\n| 1230 | 985 |\n| 1229 | 2116 |\n| 1228 | 391 |\n| 1227 | 1147 |\n| 1226 | 733 |\n| 1225 | 1582 |\n| 1224 | 1637 |\n| 1223 | 1521 |\n| 1222 | 1382 |\n| 1221 | 1364 |\n| 1220 | 2506 |\n| 1219 | 1885 |\n| 1218 | 308 |\n| 1217 | 942 |\n| 1216 | 775 |\n| 1215 | 1644 |\n| 1214 | 741 |\n| 1213 | 1869 |\n| 1212 | 353 |\n| 1211 | 885 |\n| 1210 | 832 |\n| 1209 | 3871 |\n| 1208 | 347 |\n| 1207 | 1604 |\n| 1206 | 3286 |\n| 1205 | 731 |\n| 1204 | 1879 |\n| 1203 | 1855 |\n| 1202 | 306 |\n| 1201 | 903 |\n| 1200 | 789 |\n| 1199 | 1341 |\n| 1198 | 1808 |\n| 1197 | 728 |\n| 1196 | 1851 |\n| 1195 | 323 |\n| 1194 | 980 |\n| 1193 | 725 |\n| 1192 | 1867 |\n| 1191 |\
