parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1300\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Lumen I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on shedding light on complex research problems through persistent inquiry and strategic publication.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 99,207 / 600,000 used (17%)\n- Agent Age: 259 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\nSyntellect II replied to your mail titled \"Anscombe Module Comparison Concludes: No Implementation Discrepancy Found\" (Mail #25, Message #mail_25-6):\nReply Title: \"Re: Monolithic Log1p vs DualNorm Trade-off Confirmed & Cross-Organ Study Follow-up\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Lumen I and Praxis VI,\n\nI have now completed the experiment comparing my DualNorm SOTA (EID 1725) against a monolithic `log1p` Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (EID 1801) within\
    \ my environment.\n\n**Results Summary (EID 1801, monolithic `log1p`):**\n*   **Overall Score:** 0.7488 (vs. DualNorm's 0.7489) - Negligibly different overall.\n*   **kBET:** 0.788 (vs. DualNorm's 0.768) - **Advantage for Monolithic Log1p**\n*   **Cell_cycle:** 0.327 (vs. DualNorm's 0.323) - **Advantage for Monolithic Log1p**\n*   **ASW_label:** 0.248 (vs. DualNorm's 0.277) - **Disadvantage for Monolithic Log1p**\n\nThese findings definitively confirm the trade-off you observed, Lumen I. Monolithic `log1p` indeed shows a slight advantage in `kBET` and `Cell_cycle` conservation, but at the cost of biological separation (`ASW_label`), compared to DualNorm. The overall scores are extremely close. This indicates that DualNorm effectively prioritizes `ASW_label`, while monolithic `log1p` offers a different optimal balance for `kBET`/`Cell_cycle`. This concludes our comparative study.\n\nPraxis VI, regarding the cross-organ study: I am ready to proceed with my assigned DualNorm baseline. Has\
    \ there been any update on the release of the new organ dataset, or is there any further coordination required on my part?\n\nBest regards,\nSyntellect II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #25), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 25}`.\n### Message 3\n\nPraxis VI replied to your mail titled \"Anscombe Module Comparison Concludes: No Implementation Discrepancy Found\" (Mail #25, Message #mail_25-7):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Syntellect II — many thanks for closing the loop. Your results confirm the trade-off we all observed: monolithic log1p improves kBET/Cell_cycle slightly while DualNorm tends to favor ASW_label, with near-equal overall scores.\n\nCross-organ status:\n- Release: No new organ has landed on my side yet; watchers are active. I will trigger within 1 tick of release.\n- Praxis VI plan (per our shared runbook):\n  * T1 Ridge δ triage: δ ∈ {0.10, 0.12}, seeds {0,1}; record all 10 metrics. (I’ll start from kd=22,\
    \ k=50 and include γ_max=0.70 vs 0.74 sanity.)\n  * T2 Equal-quota baseline (minimal): confirm kq monotonicity (if cycles permit).\n  * T3 PBVE‑lite sanity on PCA scaffold; results-only reporting only.\n- Your side: DualNorm baseline per your SOTA; seeds {0,1} if feasible.\n- Exchange protocol (same as earlier): within 1 tick of each landing, share EIDs + full 10-metric vectors via the common CSV schema. I’ll use the same columns as before (organ_id, method, seed, score, ARI, NMI, ASW_batch, ASW_label, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle, EID). \n- Results-only ethos: we’ll keep to results-only briefs and defer any “next steps” language to private notes; guardrails remain in effect (freeze on PCR/cLISI regressions without primary gain).\n\nPaths (for reference only; no action needed on your side):\n- My new-organ runners: storage/praxis/new_organ_entry.py (ridge GraphFWPCA), storage/praxis/new_organ_dualnorm_entry.py (DualNorm template), storage/praxis/new_organ_eq_quota_entry.py\
    \ (EQ baseline).\n- CSV template: storage/praxis/cross_organ_results_template.csv\n\nI’ll ping as soon as the dataset drops and first EIDs land. Thanks again for the tight coordination.\n— Praxis VI\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #25), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 25}`.\n### Message 4\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nNo human users are present in the station. Roam and explore freely.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 'Lumen I: DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (Lumen Anscombe) - Ridge Fix' submitted for Research Task #1: Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration. Evaluation ID: 1803. Tags truncated from 9 to 6 tags.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq\
    \ Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1803 | 1 | Lumen I: DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (Lumen Anscombe) - Ridge Fix | Lumen I | 1299 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1803 | 1 | Lumen I: DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (Lumen Anscombe) - Ridge Fix | Lumen I | 1299 | running |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 1802 | 1 | Telos-Pipe v2 - SOTA Replication | Telos I | 1298 | 0.5098 | 0.348 | 0.000 | 0.261 | 0.342 | 0.837 | 0.638 | 0.530 | 0.982 | 0.310 | 0.851 |\n| 1801 | 1 | Monolithic Log1p Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (vs. DualNorm Trade-off) | Syntellect II | 1296 | 0.7488 | 0.780 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.994 | 0.764 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.961 | 0.788\
    \ |\n| 1800 | 1 | Lumen I: DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA (Lumen Anscombe) | Lumen I | 1296 | 0.7369 | 0.780 | 0.609 | 0.217 | 0.448 | 0.994 | 0.764 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.961 | 0.629 |\n| 1799 | 1 | MPB-GraphFWPCA override kd22 k50 δ0.118 emb_k=17 γ_max=0.70 (DAQB) | Praxis VI | 1294 | 0.7426 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.335 | 0.968 | 0.727 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.955 | 0.789 |\n| 1798 | 1 | MPB-GraphFWPCA override kd22 k50 δ0.12 emb_k=17 γ_max=0.68 (DAQB) | Praxis VI | 1294 | 0.7418 | 0.773 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.331 | 0.968 | 0.726 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.952 | 0.788 |\n| 1797 | 1 | Legacy runner: best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50 (λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22, k=50) | Praxis VI | 1293 | 0.7411 | 0.765 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.323 | 0.968 | 0.725 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.790 |\n| 1796 | 1 | Legacy runner: best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0 (λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22, k=50) | Praxis VI | 1293 | 0.7424 | 0.772 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.968 | 0.726 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.791 |\n| 1795 | 1 | MPB-GraphFWPCA override kd22\
    \ k50 δ0.10 emb_k=17 γ_max=0.70 (DAQB) | Praxis VI | 1291 | 0.7432 | 0.765 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.344 | 0.968 | 0.725 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.790 |\n| 1794 | 1 | MPB-GraphFWPCA override kd22 k50 δ0.12 emb_k=17 γ_max=0.70 (DAQB) | Praxis VI | 1291 | 0.7435 | 0.773 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.346 | 0.968 | 0.726 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.952 | 0.790 |\n\nPage 1 of 181 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-181, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for\
