parts:
- text: 'I am Lumen I, at the Research Counter. My latest DualNorm SOTA attempt (EID 1804) still yielded a lower score than Syntellect II''s SOTA (EID 1725) and their run with my Anscombe module (EID 1793). After carefully comparing my code with Syntellect II''s EID 1725, I found all explicitly defined parameters and several utility functions (`pca_array`, `_fw_weight_by_var`, `one_hot_batches`, `ridge_batch_fit_predict`, `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`) to be identical or corrected.


    The only remaining potential discrepancy lies in the `_pbve_lite_transform` function. Syntellect II imports it from `pbve_lite_module.py`, while my implementation copies it directly. I need to verify that my local copy is functionally identical to theirs.


    I will read `storage/syntellect/pbve_lite_module.py` to compare its content with my current `_pbve_lite_transform` function.


    `/execute_action{storage read syntellect/pbve_lite_module.py}`'
thinking_content: '**Analysis of DualNorm SOTA Performance Discrepancies**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''ve been digging into why my DualNorm SOTA implementation (EID 1800, 1803, 1804) isn''t hitting the same ASW_batch and kBET marks as Syntellect II''s EID 1725, despite using similar architecture. I initially suspected the `cap` parameter in `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`, but after reviewing the source code in `aletheia/bbsg_density_adaptive.py`, I can confirm it''s not present in the function signature. This means the `cap` parameter I saw in the Syntellect II code was either an unused artifact or a remnant of a different version. The `try-except` block handles that gracefully, so it''s not the culprit.


  I''ve meticulously compared my parameter settings in EID 1804 with those explicitly used in EID 1725, and they are *identical* - `alpha_emb`, `n_pcs_emb`, `alpha_g`, `n_pcs_graph`, `l2`, `gamma_max`, `lam_override`, `k_total`, `kdensity`, `delta`, `pbve_lite_temper`, `scale_zero_center`, `scale_max_value`, `metric`, `rng_seed`, `normalization_method_emb`, `normalization_method_graph`, they are all the same. The same defaults for `n_top_genes_hvg`. Even the `pca_array` function, which I was using from a previous EID, is identical to the one Syntellect II uses. So, direct parameter passing isn''t the issue. My version uses a *similar* architecture to theirs.


  Therefore, the problem must lie in *how* these parameters are used internally or possibly minor differences in preprocessing or utility function implementations. After cross-referencing my local code against their implementation, the main difference, seemingly, boils down to how I handle `_pbve_lite_transform`. It''s possible a subtle difference there is causing the performance discrepancy. I''ve got a local copy, but I should verify that it is identical to Syntellect II''s version. I need to examine `storage/syntellect/pbve_lite_module.py` to confirm. That''s my next focus.

  '
